{"page_content": "Analysis of the data provided by 2010 and 2013 registrants\n\nAn analysis initiated by JRC (Graepel et a/., 2016) and then continued by ECHA has shown that, for substances of low toxicity, the prediction of acute oral toxicity classification can be based on the data from oral sub-acute studies in most cases.\n\nThe data used for this analysis were extracted in May 2015 by ECHA, from the whole REACH registration database, from studies reported in sections 7.2.1 (Acute toxicity: oral) and 7.5.1 (Repeated dose toxicity: oral) of the IUCLID dossiers.\n\nStep 1: A preliminary set of filters was used to select the relevant experimental data:\n\ne \u201cTest material identity same as registered substance\u201d = \u201cyes\u201d;\n\ne \u201cStudy type\u201d = \u201cexperimental result\u201d (to select only experimental data and to exclude other study types such read-across or QSAR results);\n\ne Reliability score = \u201c1\u201d or \u201c2\u201d.\n\nStep 2: An additional filter was used to select the relevant studies performed according to the following EU/OECD guidelines:\n\ne for acute toxicity: LDso values from EU Method B.1 (bis and tris) or OECD TG 401, 420, 423 or 425;\n\ne for repeated dose toxicity: NOAEL or NOEL from OECD TG 407 or 422, excluding results expressed only in ppm.\n\nStep 3: Another filter was used to select only dossiers containing relevant studies in both 7.2.1 and 7.5.1 sections.\n\nAs a result, 1256 registration dossiers were selected.\n\nIn the remaining registration dossiers, other routes of administration (often inhalation) have been used for the acute and/or sub-acute toxicity tests, or one of these studies was adapted, e.g. by using information on an analogue substance (i.e. read-across adaptation). Hence these study record(s) in the IUCLID dossier could not be used for this analysis.\n\nStep 4: Refinement; ECHA then refined the data set as follows:\n\ne Exclude sub-acute studies reporting a NOAEL < 1000 mg/kg bw;\n\ne If a range was given for a single study, the lowest value was selected;\n\ne If the registrant submitted more than one relevant study per endpoint, the study resulting in the lowest LDso value and/or lowest NOAEL value was selected;\n\ne Furthermore, the information on the identity of the test material was checked in order to exclude cases where another substance than the registered substance could have been tested (i.e. \u201chidden\u201d read-across)98.\n\nTo summarise, the data included in the final prediction model include dossiers with:\n\ne Relevant acute oral and sub-acute oral toxicity /screening study tests?9; and\n\ne Sub-acute oral toxicity study which resulted in a NOAEL at or above 1000 mg/kg bw.\n\nPlease note that registrant self-classification was not considered.\n\nSubstances in 415 dossiers fulfil the above criteria. In addition, all except nine dossiers gave an acute oral toxicity study with an LD50 higher than 2000 mg/kg bw. These cases were manually analysed, and explanations included e.g. the differences in units used or the different modes of administration of the oral dose between the acute and repeated dose studies.\n\nIn conclusion, this \u201cprediction model\u201d based on sub-acute toxicity data can be used and constitutes the core element of the WoE approach.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "fa80e24d-3cb7-4c4d-bf7b-c088eecde1ad", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "information_requirements_r7a_en_part_3.pdf"}}